## The core idea
The URL is the oldest state container on the web, and for a large class of UI state it is the best one. If a user would reasonably expect **refresh, the back button, a bookmark or a pasted link** to bring them back to the same view, that state belongs in the URL: search query, filters, sort order, page, selected tab, open item id, map viewport, date range.

Putting it anywhere else (useState, a store, localStorage) silently breaks things users rely on: they share a link to "open bugs assigned to me, sorted by priority" and the recipient sees the default list; they press back after opening an item and leave the page entirely. URL state also makes **server rendering** straightforward, because the server receives the state with the request.

## What belongs in the URL
| Belongs in the URL | Does not |
| --- | --- |
| Search text (after debounce), filters, sort, page or cursor | Secrets, tokens, personal data (URLs end up in logs, analytics, Referer headers and browser history) |
| Selected tab or view mode when it is a meaningful "place" | Hover, focus, transient open/close of a tooltip |
| Id of the open item, modal or drawer that should be linkable | Large blobs (whole form drafts, big JSON) |
| Date range, map centre and zoom | High-frequency values (scroll position, slider while dragging) |

Two kinds of URL state:

- **Path** for identity and hierarchy: `/projects/42/issues/1337`. Changing it is navigation to a different resource.
- **Search params** for views of a resource: `/projects/42/issues?status=open&sort=-priority&page=2`. Changing them refines the same page.

## How it actually works
```text
user changes a filter
      |
serialize state -> URLSearchParams ("status=open&sort=-priority")
      |
history.replaceState / pushState  (or router.replace / router.push)
      |                                   \
URL changes, no full page load             browser history entry added (push) or replaced (replace)
      |
router notifies subscribers -> components read params -> parse + validate -> render
      |
(optional) server re-renders with the new searchParams (Next.js non-shallow navigation)

back button -> popstate -> router re-reads URL -> same parse path -> UI restored
```

Key decisions:

- **push vs replace.** Push creates a history entry, so back undoes it. Replace changes the URL without an entry. Use **push** for discrete, meaningful changes (choosing a tab, applying a filter set, paginating) and **replace** for continuous ones (typing in a search box, dragging a map), otherwise the back button steps through every keystroke.
- **Parse and validate on read.** URL params are untrusted user input. `?page=banana` or `?sort=drop table` must fall back to defaults, not crash.
- **Canonical, minimal URLs.** Omit params equal to their default so the default view has a clean URL and caches well.

## Real code: Next.js App Router without libraries
```tsx
"use client";
import { usePathname, useRouter, useSearchParams } from "next/navigation";
import { useTransition } from "react";
import { z } from "zod";

const Filters = z.object({
  status: z.enum(["all", "open", "closed"]).catch("all"),
  sort: z.enum(["-created", "created", "-priority"]).catch("-created"),
  page: z.coerce.number().int().min(1).catch(1),
});
type Filters = z.infer<typeof Filters>;
const DEFAULTS: Filters = { status: "all", sort: "-created", page: 1 };

export function useFilters() {
  const params = useSearchParams();
  const router = useRouter();
  const pathname = usePathname();
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  const filters = Filters.parse(Object.fromEntries(params)); // .catch() makes bad values fall back

  function setFilters(patch: Partial<Filters>, mode: "push" | "replace" = "push") {
    const next = { ...filters, ...patch, ...(patch.page === undefined ? { page: 1 } : {}) }; // reset page on filter change
    const sp = new URLSearchParams(params);
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(next)) {
      if (v === DEFAULTS[k as keyof Filters]) sp.delete(k);
      else sp.set(k, String(v));
    }
    const url = sp.size ? `${pathname}?${sp}` : pathname;
    startTransition(() => (mode === "push" ? router.push(url, { scroll: false }) : router.replace(url, { scroll: false })));
  }
  return { filters, setFilters, isPending };
}
```

And on the server, the page reads the same params:

```tsx
// app/issues/page.tsx (Server Component). searchParams is a Promise in Next.js 15+.
export default async function IssuesPage({ searchParams }: { searchParams: Promise<Record<string, string | string[] | undefined>> }) {
  const raw = await searchParams;
  const filters = Filters.parse(raw);
  const issues = await listIssues(filters);
  return <IssueTable issues={issues} />;
}
```

Notes:

- Resetting `page` to 1 when a filter changes avoids "page 7 of 2" empty results.
- `useTransition` keeps the old list visible (with a pending indicator) while the server renders the new one.
- In the App Router, `router.push` with new search params re-renders Server Components for that route. If the change is purely client-side (for example, which column is highlighted), `window.history.replaceState(null, "", url)` updates the URL and Next's `useSearchParams` without a server round-trip; Next.js integrates native `pushState`/`replaceState` with its router.
- `useSearchParams` in a statically rendered route makes the component render on the client up to the nearest `Suspense` boundary; wrap it in `Suspense` or the build warns.

## nuqs-style patterns
Hand-rolling the parse/serialise/defaults code for every page gets repetitive. **nuqs** is a small library that makes a search param behave like `useState`, with typed parsers:

```tsx
"use client";
import { parseAsInteger, parseAsStringLiteral, parseAsString, useQueryStates } from "nuqs";

const statusValues = ["all", "open", "closed"] as const;

export function useIssueFilters() {
  return useQueryStates(
    {
      q: parseAsString.withDefault(""),
      status: parseAsStringLiteral(statusValues).withDefault("all"),
      page: parseAsInteger.withDefault(1),
    },
    { history: "push" },
  );
}

// in a component
const [{ q, status, page }, setFilters] = useIssueFilters();
setFilters({ status: "open", page: 1 });
```

Defaults worth knowing: nuqs uses `history: "replace"` unless you ask for push, updates are **shallow** by default in Next.js (client only, no Server Component re-render) unless you pass `shallow: false`, values equal to the default are removed from the URL by default (configurable), and URL writes are throttled because browsers rate-limit the History API. It also has server-side helpers to parse the same params in Server Components with the same parsers.

TanStack Router takes the idea further: search params are declared per route with a validation schema and are fully typed in links and navigation.

## Routing state beyond search params
- **Modals as routes**: an issue drawer at `/issues?open=1337`, or with Next.js intercepting and parallel routes, `/issues/1337` that renders as a modal over the list when navigated from the list, and as a full page on refresh or direct visit. Users can share the link and the back button closes the modal.
- **Scroll restoration**: the browser restores scroll on back/forward if content is ready; lists that load data async need cached data (a query cache helps) so the page height exists when restoration happens.
- **Hash fragments** (`#section-3`) are never sent to the server; fine for in-page anchors, not for state the server must render.

## Debouncing search
Typing "kubernetes" should not create ten history entries or ten requests.

```tsx
const [{ q }, setFilters] = useIssueFilters();
const [input, setInput] = useState(q);           // local, instant
useEffect(() => setInput(q), [q]);               // back button updates the box
useEffect(() => {
  const t = setTimeout(() => { if (input !== q) setFilters({ q: input || null, page: 1 }); }, 300);
  return () => clearTimeout(t);
}, [input]);                                       // URL updates after 300 ms idle
```

The input is local state for responsiveness; the URL is the committed state. On back/forward, the URL drives the input. Use `replace` for these updates.

## Trade-offs and when not to use the URL
- **Length**: browsers handle long URLs, but proxies, CDNs, logs and servers often cap request lines; keep URLs compact and avoid serialising large structures. If a filter set is huge, save it server-side and put its id in the URL.
- **Privacy**: anything in the URL is visible in history, server logs, analytics and may leak via `Referer` to third parties (mitigated with a `Referrer-Policy`, but do not rely on it for secrets).
- **Caching**: each distinct query string is a distinct cache key at the CDN; normalise param order and drop defaults to avoid fragmenting the cache.
- **Server cost**: non-shallow updates in Next.js re-render on the server; use shallow updates for client-only state.
- **SEO**: faceted URLs can create near-infinite crawlable pages; use canonical links and `noindex` on combinations you do not want indexed.

## Common mistakes and debugging
- **Duplicating URL state into useState** and letting them drift; read from the URL, keep local state only for the in-progress input.
- **Not validating params** and crashing on `?page=-1`.
- **Push on every keystroke**, making the back button useless.
- **Forgetting to reset pagination** when filters change.
- **Building query strings by concatenation** without encoding; always use `URLSearchParams`, which encodes values for you.
- **Debugging**: watch the URL bar and the history stack (press back a few times); in DevTools, check whether filter changes fire a server request (Next.js RSC fetches show as `?_rsc=` requests) when you expected a client-only update.

